# What you type at the shell, before you are inside tmux. `tmux --help` prints
# one usage line and nothing else, so there is nothing worth probing here —
# every row is literal, and the keys view is the one that reads the live config.
name:    tmux
what:    starting, listing and re-attaching sessions from the shell
check:   tmux
install: sudo dnf install tmux   /   sudo apt install tmux
docs:    man tmux

= STARTING
tmux             | start a session
tmux new -s work | start one with a name you will recognise later
tmux new -A -s work | attach to `work` if it exists, start it if it does not

= GETTING BACK IN
tmux a           | re-attach to the last session after an ssh drop
tmux a -t work   | re-attach to a named one
tmux ls          | list what is running
tmux kill-session -t work | end one for good
tmux kill-server | end all of them

= FROM OUTSIDE, INTO A SESSION
tmux send-keys -t work "make test" Enter | type into a session you are not in
tmux capture-pane -pt work | print what a pane currently shows
tmux list-keys   | every binding, without being attached
tmux show-options -g | every global option, with its current value

= CONFIG
tmux source-file ~/.tmux.conf | reload after an edit, without restarting
tmux -f FILE     | run with a different config, for trying one out
tmux -V          | the version, which decides which options exist
